window.SI_PAPERS = [{"id":0,"title":"Planetary Information Processing and Memory Storage Through Deep Time: A Bounded Order-of-Magnitude Reconstruction from Prebiotic Chemistry to the Modern Technosphere","plain_title":"Counting a planet's memory and computing","thesis":"Count everything on Earth that stores or processes information, from the first cells to today's data centers, keep each kind in its own units, and four findings stand out.","summary":"The paper builds a census of Earth across 68 moments over 4.10 billion years. At each moment it estimates ten separate quantities: three kinds of DNA storage, two kinds of memory, three kinds of event rate (cell chemistry, nerve signals, chip operations), total power, and how long information persists. Today's values are built bottom-up from published counts of cells, animals and devices across 975 cataloged systems, with uncertainty ranges from 10,000 Monte Carlo draws. Older values are scaled back from those anchors using fossils and rock chemistry.","claims":[{"claim":"Life and technology together use a fixed, countable amount of power.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; Table B1","paper_value":"65.0 TW for life plus 19.5 TW for technology, 84.5 TW in all","data_value":"65.0 TW, 19.5 TW and 84.5 TW","source":"Power ledger (paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv): compartments added up","verdict":"matches","note":"The six compartments in the ledger sum to the stated totals."},{"claim":"Life uses about half of what plants capture from sunlight.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"52% of 125 TW","data_value":"52.0% (65.0 ÷ 125)","source":"Census value for life's power; the 125 TW figure is cited from outside literature","verdict":"matches","note":"The division is right; the 125 TW primary-production anchor comes from published biogeochemistry."},{"claim":"Microbial power and carbon divide cleanly into deep-underground, standby and active surface cells.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; Section 2.4; Table B1","paper_value":"77.5 Gt C at 25 fg C per cell (17.25, 48.625 and 11.625 Gt C); 0.69 TW, 3.89 TW and 9.42 TW, totalling 14.00 TW at 10, 150 and 5,000 Hz","data_value":"17.25, 48.625 and 11.625 Gt C; 0.69 TW, 3.89 TW and 9.42 TW, totalling 14.00 TW; 10, 150 and 5,000 Hz","source":"Power ledger, rows 1a to 1c","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: Section 2.4, Appendix A.1/C.1 and Table B1 all use the uniform 25 fg C per cell and identical per-compartment rates."},{"claim":"The number of microbial cells follows from their total carbon.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; Section 2.1","paper_value":"3.10×10³⁰ cells from 77.5 billion tonnes of carbon at 25 femtograms per cell","data_value":"3.10×10³⁰ cells","source":"Census: microbial carbon divided by carbon per cell","verdict":"matches","note":"77.5×10¹⁵ g ÷ 25×10⁻¹⁵ g gives 3.10×10³⁰."},{"claim":"Cell chemistry is the largest event rate on the planet.","section":"Abstract, finding 1; Table MC1","paper_value":"2.80×10³³ events a second deterministic central (median 2.70×10³³, 95% range 9.46×10³² to 8.25×10³³)","data_value":"2.80×10³³ events a second (median 2.70×10³³, 95% range 9.46×10³² to 8.25×10³³)","source":"Census and the uncertainty ledger (paper0_monte_carlo_summary.csv)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: Table MC1 prints both the exact deterministic value and the Monte Carlo median alongside the 95% interval."},{"claim":"All the DNA in living cells can be counted in bits.","section":"Abstract, finding 1","paper_value":"3.60×10³⁷ bits in total, 1.84×10³⁷ under selection, 3.80×10¹² unique","data_value":"3.60×10³⁷, 1.84×10³⁷ and 3.80×10¹² bits","source":"Census, 2026 row","verdict":"matches","note":"All three values match the 2026 census row as stated."},{"claim":"Every animal nervous system together fires a countable number of signals.","section":"Abstract, finding 2; Table MC1","paper_value":"3.43×10²⁴ neurons, 3.00×10²⁷ synapses, 3.00×10²⁶ signals a second (95% range 9.08×10²⁵ to 9.92×10²⁶)","data_value":"3.43×10²⁴ neurons, 3.00×10²⁷ synapses, 3.00×10²⁶ signals a second (95% range 9.08×10²⁵ to 9.92×10²⁶)","source":"Census; synapses × 0.10 signals a second each; uncertainty ledger","verdict":"matches","note":"Text, Table MC1 and Figures 1–3 all plot and cite the exact 95% Monte Carlo range."},{"claim":"Two mass extinctions each removed more than nine tenths of nerve signalling.","section":"Abstract, finding 2; Section 4.2","paper_value":"94.0% at the end of the Permian (95% range 87.8% to 99.0%), 92.5% at the end-Cretaceous asteroid impact (95% range 84.4% to 98.5%)","data_value":"94.0% [87.8%, 99.0%] and 92.5% [84.4%, 98.5%]","source":"Census: change between the moments either side of each extinction; uncertainty ledger","verdict":"matches","note":"Both drops recompute exactly from the 68-moment census as constrained paleobiological scenarios."},{"claim":"All the world's chips together execute a countable number of operations.","section":"Abstract, finding 3; Table D1; Table MC1","paper_value":"1.89×10²² executed operations a second (95% range 9.31×10²¹ to 3.24×10²²; peak 7.60×10²²), 9.00×10²¹ of them on AI accelerators, drawing 80.0 GW","data_value":"1.89×10²² executed operations a second (95% range 9.31×10²¹ to 3.24×10²²; peak 7.60×10²²), 9.00×10²¹ on AI accelerators, 80.0 GW","source":"Device ledger (paper0_device_inventory_ledger.csv) and uncertainty ledger","verdict":"matches","note":"All four device classes and the updated 95% range match the device and Monte Carlo ledgers."},{"claim":"Chips remain far below nervous systems when everything is added up.","section":"Abstract, finding 3; Section 2.4","paper_value":"1.59×10⁴ times below all animals (ratio 6.30×10⁻⁵, 95% range 1.57×10⁻⁵ to 2.23×10⁻⁴) and 64.3 times below all 8.1 billion human brains (1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations per second at 162 GW)","data_value":"1.59×10⁴ times below all animals and 64.3 times below 8.1 billion human brains (162 GW)","source":"Census: nerve signals ÷ chip operations; 8.1×10⁹ people × 1.50×10¹⁴ signals each ÷ 1.89×10²²","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: human population is standardised to 8.1 billion across all sections and human brain power to 162 GW (0.162 TW)."},{"claim":"One large AI cluster out-runs one human brain in raw operations per second.","section":"Abstract, finding 3","paper_value":"3.00×10²⁰ operations against 1.50×10¹⁴ signals a second: 2.0 million times","data_value":"2.0 million times","source":"Census: largest single system today ÷ one human brain","verdict":"matches","note":"The division is exact and explicitly contrasts chip operations with synaptic events."},{"claim":"Modern storage holds 15 zettabytes with an 8.30-year unpowered mean lifetime and 9.96×10²³ bit-years of persistence.","section":"Abstract, finding 4; Table P1; Table 1","paper_value":"1.20×10²³ bits installed; weighted mean unpowered retention 8.30 years (flash 3.5, hard disks 7.0, tape 25); 9.96×10²³ bit-years unpowered; 20.0 GW refresh power (9 GW storage scrubbing + 11 GW DRAM refresh)","data_value":"1.20×10²³ bits, 8.30 years, 9.96×10²³ bit-years and 20.0 GW across both the memory ledger and the 2026 census row","source":"Memory ledger (paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv) and the 68-moment census","verdict":"matches","note":"Both the memory ledger and the 68-row Table 1 / S1 close identically to 9.96×10²³ bit-years."},{"claim":"The reconstruction spans 68 census moments calibrated on 975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources and 26 parameter anchors.","section":"Abstract; Section 1; Appendix Table S0","paper_value":"68 sub-epochs, 975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources, 26 parameter-register anchors","data_value":"68 sub-epochs, 975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources, 26 parameter-register anchors","source":"Catalog workbook, planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv, and paper0_source_to_parameter_register.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: manuscript, appendix and figures include all four newly added evolutionary and historical stops."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Keep ten quantities in their own physical units and never merge them into one composite score.","assessment":"holds","note":"Bits, events a second, watts and bit-years remain strictly separated across all 68 census moments."},{"step":"Build today's values bottom-up from published counts and require carbon, cells, devices and power to close.","assessment":"holds","note":"Every compartment in the text, Table B1, Table D1, Table P1 and the 2026 census row closes identically."},{"step":"Scale back through deep time from today's anchors using fossils, rock chemistry and living relatives.","assessment":"weak","note":"There is no direct fossil meter for ancient firing rates, so pre-modern moments remain bounded order-of-magnitude scenarios, as the paper explicitly states."},{"step":"Compare cell chemistry, nerve signals and chip operations by counting elementary state transitions per second.","assessment":"weak","note":"Counting transitions per second is physically well defined, though one enzymatic reaction, one synaptic release and one 16-bit multiply are different kinds of work."},{"step":"Propagate parameter uncertainty across 10,000 Monte Carlo draws.","assessment":"holds","note":"Deterministic central values, medians and 95% intervals now agree across the abstract, Table MC1 and all figures."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"The world's chips have not overtaken the world's nervous systems in aggregate event rate.","assessment":"supported","note":"The joint 95% interval of the chip-to-nerve ratio runs from 1.57×10⁻⁵ to 2.23×10⁻⁴, staying four orders of magnitude below parity."},{"conclusion":"One frontier AI cluster exceeds one human brain by two million times in raw operations per second.","assessment":"partly","note":"Exact as a raw rate ratio (3.00×10²⁰ ÷ 1.50×10¹⁴), while leaving functional equivalence per operation open."},{"conclusion":"Mass extinctions acted as severe bottlenecks that erased over nine tenths of animal nerve signalling before mammal and bird radiations.","assessment":"partly","note":"The drops (94.0% and 92.5%) follow cleanly from the paleobiological biomass model, though the ecological release step remains an evolutionary interpretation."},{"conclusion":"Modern digital storage holds vastly more bits than ancient archives yet requires 20 GW of continuous power to avoid decay over an 8.3-year unpowered horizon.","assessment":"supported","note":"Fully verified across the memory persistence and power closure ledgers."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 9 November and 24 November 2026 revisions, all 68 census moments, 975-system catalog counts, prokaryotic carbon and rate compartments, 8.1-billion human totals (162 GW, 64.3× chip gap), Monte Carlo deterministic centrals and 95% intervals, unpowered storage persistence (9.96×10²³ bit-years) and figures agree across the manuscript, appendix and canonical CSV ledgers. Pre-modern deep-time rows remain bounded order-of-magnitude scenarios rather than direct measurements.","revised":"On 9 November 2026, six errors in power units, budget closure, uncertainty intervals, device splits and refresh power were corrected. On 24 November 2026, the manuscript, appendix and figures were expanded to all 68 census moments and 975 cataloged systems, and prokaryotic carbon per cell, the 8.1-billion human baseline, Table MC1 deterministic centrals and the 2026 persistence product (9.96×10²³ bit-years) were unified across every table and ledger.","pdf":"papers/paper0.pdf","corrected_docx":"papers/paper0-corrected.docx","pdf_stale":false},{"id":1,"title":"Physical Constraints on Latency and Energy Use in Biological and Artificial Information Processing","plain_title":"How fast and how cheaply can brains and chips compute?","thesis":"Brains and computers are governed by the same transport delays and heat limits, and when you compare on-chip compute to local synapses the energy gap is about 71 times — whereas moving data off-chip to DRAM opens a 20,000-fold chasm.","summary":"The paper benchmarks all 33 physical and biological substrates in the project's hardware table across 68 census moments — from bacterial chemotaxis, cortical synapses and giant squid axons to Nvidia GPUs, photonic accelerators and 4.2-kelvin superconducting logic. It separates ballistic and cable propagation from square-law chemical diffusion, shows why the Landauer bit-erasure floor sits 19 orders of magnitude faster than millisecond neural integration, compares local synaptic currents (0.01 pJ) and amortized whole-brain synaptic budgets (0.133 pJ) against CMOS and cryogenic hardware, and evaluates neural power and brain-body scaling directly on the project's empirical tables.","claims":[{"claim":"A local cortical postsynaptic event is 70.9 times more energy efficient than a dense 16-bit operation on an Nvidia H100 — and 20,000 times more efficient than an off-chip HBM3 DRAM fetch.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.4; Table 1; Figure 2","paper_value":"local synapse 0.010 pJ (1.00×10¹⁴ events/J; whole-brain amortized human budget 0.133 pJ = 7.50×10¹² SynOps/J) vs H100 dense 1.41×10¹² ops/J (70.9× local, 5.32× amortized), B200 4.50×10¹² (22.2×), Apple M4 NPU 7.60×10¹² (13.2× local, 0.99× amortized parity), and HBM3 DRAM fetch 5.00×10⁹ ops/J (20,000× local)","data_value":"70.9× vs H100 dense, 22.2× vs B200, 13.2× vs M4 NPU, and 20,000× vs HBM3 DRAM in cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (cortical synapse, H100 dense, B200, M4 NPU, and HBM3 DRAM rows); Attwell and Laughlin 2001","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: both the local postsynaptic cleft cost (0.010 pJ) and the whole-brain amortized synaptic cost (0.133 pJ) are reported alongside the exact 20,000× DRAM ratio."},{"claim":"Superconducting AQFP logic cooled to 4.2 kelvin still beats every biological and room-temperature substrate after paying a ~400-to-1 Carnot + cryocooler wall-plug penalty.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.4; Supplementary Note S2","paper_value":"7.14×10¹⁹ ops/J intrinsic at 4.2 K; 1.79×10¹⁷ ops/J wall-plug after 400× cooling overhead = 1,790× above a local cortical synapse","data_value":"Carnot + 17.5% cryocooler efficiency gives ~402 W cooling per W cold dissipation; 7.14×10¹⁹ ÷ 400 = 1.79×10¹⁷ ops/J; ÷ 1.00×10¹⁴ = 1,790×","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (AQFP intrinsic and wall-plug rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Every step of the cryogenic Carnot chain and the 1,790× lead over cortical synapses reproduces cleanly."},{"claim":"Wall-plug cooled AQFP logic exceeds the most efficient room-temperature CMOS chips in the benchmark table by 2.36×10⁴ to 1.27×10⁵ times (4.4 to 5.1 orders of magnitude).","section":"Abstract; Section 4.4; Figure 2","paper_value":"2.36×10⁴× vs Apple M4 NPU (7.60×10¹² ops/J), 2.33×10⁴× vs Intel Hala Point (7.69×10¹²), 3.98×10⁴× vs Nvidia B200 (4.50×10¹²), and 1.27×10⁵× vs Nvidia H100 (1.41×10¹²)","data_value":"1.79×10¹⁷ ÷ 7.60×10¹² = 2.36×10⁴; ÷ 4.50×10¹² = 3.98×10⁴; ÷ 1.41×10¹² = 1.27×10⁵","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: ratios are now given explicitly against the M4 NPU, Hala Point, B200 and H100 rather than treating H100 as the room-temperature CMOS ceiling."},{"claim":"Table 1 prints all 33 substrates verbatim from the canonical hardware benchmark table, including the electric-fish pacemaker cycle, dragonfly target-selective neuron and Apple M4 Neural Engine.","section":"Section 4.2; Table 1","paper_value":"all 33 rows printed verbatim: electric fish 2.0 mm, 1.0 ms full 1 kHz cycle (vs 0.16–0.20 µs phase jitter in text), 5.00×10⁹ ops/J; dragonfly TSDN 4.0 mm, 25 ms, 8.00×10⁶ ops/J; Apple M4 NPU 5.0 W, 0.444 ns, 7.60×10¹² ops/J (7.6 TOPS/W at 1 MAC/op)","data_value":"exact row-for-row and column-for-column match across all 33 rows of cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv (all 33 rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: all 33 substrates appear in Table 1 and match the CSV identically."},{"claim":"Across all 33 substrates, operational latency spans 14.8 orders of magnitude and energy efficiency spans 14.2 orders of magnitude.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.1","paper_value":"14.81 orders in latency (2.13×10⁻¹¹ s RSFQ to 1.36×10⁴ s DNA Fountain) and 14.22 orders in efficiency (4.35×10⁵ ops/J blowfly haltere reflex to 7.14×10¹⁹ ops/J intrinsic AQFP; 11.6 orders wall-plug)","data_value":"log10(1.36×10⁴ ÷ 2.13×10⁻¹¹) = 14.81; log10(7.14×10¹⁹ ÷ 4.35×10⁵) = 14.22","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv min and max across all 33 rows","verdict":"matches","note":"Both dynamic ranges match the full 33-row benchmark file to two decimal places."},{"claim":"Pure Fickian glutamate diffusion across a 20-nanometre synaptic cleft takes 0.263 microseconds, whereas the 200-to-300-microsecond canonical synaptic delay reflects vesicle exocytosis and receptor gating.","section":"Section 2.2; Figure 1","paper_value":"τ_diff = (20 nm)² ÷ (2 × 0.76 µm²/ms) = 0.263 µs pure cleft transit, embedded inside ~200–300 µs total Ca²⁺-SNARE release and AMPA gating delay","data_value":"(2×10⁻⁸ m)² ÷ (2 × 7.6×10⁻¹⁰ m²/s) = 2.63×10⁻⁷ s = 0.263 µs; hardware row transport/gating delay 2.0×10⁻⁴ s","source":"Analytical Fickian diffusion equation and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv synapse row","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: pure molecular cleft transit (0.263 µs) and vesicular release kinetics (200 µs) are cleanly separated in both prose and Figure 1."},{"claim":"Across the 23 canonical individual nervous systems in the neural evolution table, whole-brain metabolic power scales nearly linearly with neuron count (exponent 1.04 ± 0.08), matching the 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ W per mammalian neuron budget.","section":"Abstract; Section 3.4.2; Figure 3","paper_value":"log10(P_brain) = (−9.23 ± 0.68) + (1.042 ± 0.083) × log10(N_neurons), R² = 0.882 across N = 23 canonical nervous systems; mammalian linear rule 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ W/neuron × 8.6×10¹⁰ = 19.8 W human brain","data_value":"OLS on neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv (N = 23): slope 1.042 ± 0.083, intercept −9.23 ± 0.68, R² = 0.882; 8.6×10¹⁰ × 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ = 19.78 W","source":"neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv and Herculano-Houzel 2011","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: regressed directly on neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv and paired with Herculano-Houzel's measured 230 picowatts per mammalian neuron."},{"claim":"Across the 176 individual animal species in the biological catalog, brain mass scales with body mass (in grams) with slope 0.886 ± 0.029 (R² = 0.840).","section":"Section 5.2; Figure 4","paper_value":"N = 176 individual animals: log10(M_brain,g) = −2.0502 + (0.8859 ± 0.0293) × log10(M_body,g), R² = 0.8402 (all N = 277 positive-mass rows including collectives and synthetic rows: slope 0.7955 ± 0.0411, intercept −1.9630, R² = 0.5764)","data_value":"identical slopes, intercepts, standard errors and R² values recomputed from cleaned_biological_species.csv","source":"cleaned_biological_species.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: text, stats macros and Figure 4 all report the exact gram-unit OLS fits for both the filtered 176-animal set and the full 277-row table."},{"claim":"Prokaryotic single-cell power spans five orders of magnitude across metabolic states, from 556 femtowatts in rich-medium dividing E. coli down to 1 attowatt in deep subseafloor sediments.","section":"Section 3.4.4","paper_value":"5.56×10⁻¹³ W (rich-medium dividing E. coli, 10⁻⁹ J ÷ 1,800 s), 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ W (chemotaxis rotary motor + receptor cluster), 2.03×10⁻¹⁷ W (field surface prokaryote mean), 2.00×10⁻¹⁸ W (oligotrophic standby), and 1.00×10⁻¹⁸ W (deep subseafloor basal maintenance)","data_value":"1.0×10⁻⁹ ÷ 1,800 = 5.56×10⁻¹³ W; 1.0×10⁻¹⁵ W in cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv; 2.03×10⁻¹⁷, 2.00×10⁻¹⁸ and 1.00×10⁻¹⁸ W in paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv","source":"Lynch and Marinov 2015; Hoehler and Jørgensen 2013; cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv; paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Distinguishing rich-medium division, sensory-motor chemotaxis and the three Paper 0 field compartments closes the prokaryotic bioenergetics cleanly."},{"claim":"Paracrine calcium waves across a 1-millimetre placozoan (Trichoplax) at 150 micrometres per second require 6.7 seconds for whole-body coordination, whereas myelinated vertebrate axons cut metre-scale transit to milliseconds.","section":"Section 2.2; Section 5.1","paper_value":"1.0 mm ÷ 0.15 mm/s = 6.7 s across a whole Trichoplax (0.67 s across a 100 µm patch), vs ~10 ms along a 1 m myelinated axon at 100 m/s","data_value":"1.0 ÷ 0.15 = 6.67 s; 1.0 m ÷ 100 m/s = 0.010 s","source":"Analytical kinematics and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"All transport times now follow distance ÷ speed consistently across prose and figures."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Signal latency is bounded below by path length divided by propagation speed (or length squared over diffusivity for passive chemical diffusion).","assessment":"holds","note":"Every example in the text, Table 1 and Figure 1 — cleft diffusion (0.263 µs), placozoan waves (6.7 s), electric-fish cycle (1.0 ms) and axons — obeys the transport equations."},{"step":"At 300 K, the Landauer bit-erasure floor sits ~11 orders of magnitude in energy below a cortical synapse and 19 orders in export time below millisecond neural integration, so practical limits come from capacitance, ionic pumps and heat removal.","assessment":"holds","note":"Both 2.87×10⁻²¹ J and 1.44×10⁻²² s (19 orders below 1 ms) reproduce accurately."},{"step":"Comparing on-chip 16-bit compute to a local synapse yields a 71× gap (5.3× against whole-brain amortized cost), whereas off-chip DRAM movement opens a 20,000× gap.","assessment":"holds","note":"Separating arithmetic energy from memory-traffic energy explains why data movement, not transistor switching alone, dominates AI power."},{"step":"Adiabatic superconducting logic at 4.2 K achieves a 1,790× wall-plug efficiency lead over synapses and a 24,000× to 127,000× lead over room-temperature CMOS after paying the 400× refrigeration tax.","assessment":"holds","note":"Verified against every CMOS row in the 33-substrate table, while noting cryogenic laboratory maturity limits."},{"step":"Across animal species, whole-brain power scales nearly isometrically with neuron count (~230 pW per mammalian neuron) while pooled brain-body allometry masks the clade grade shifts analysed in Paper 2.","assessment":"holds","note":"Both regressions reproduce directly from neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv and cleaned_biological_species.csv."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"On-chip GPU arithmetic sits within 71-fold of local cortical synaptic energy (and 5.3-fold of amortized whole-brain synaptic cost), whereas off-chip DRAM movement is 20,000 times costlier than a synapse.","assessment":"supported","note":"Directly verified in cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv, with operation-type differences explicitly noted."},{"conclusion":"Wall-plug 4.2 K adiabatic superconducting logic beats cortical synapses by 1,790× and the best room-temperature CMOS accelerators by 2.4×10⁴× to 1.3×10⁵× in laboratory circuit benchmarks.","assessment":"supported","note":"Follows directly from the 400× Carnot-cryocooler penalty and the 33-substrate table."},{"conclusion":"Myelination, saltatory conduction and 3D cortical folding evolved under strict transport-time and wiring-volume physics rather than Landauer bit-erasure limits.","assessment":"supported","note":"All physical time constants and geometric bounds check out cleanly."},{"conclusion":"Whole-brain metabolic power tracks total neuron count nearly linearly across animals, rising from ~0.06% to 20% of resting body metabolism (~312-fold).","assessment":"supported","note":"Reproduced on the 23-system neural power dataset and Herculano-Houzel 2011."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 24 November 2026 revision, all 33 rows of Table 1, the 14.8-order latency and 14.2-order efficiency spans, synaptic-to-GPU/DRAM/AQFP efficiency ratios, Fickian cleft diffusion (0.263 µs), Landauer exponents (19 orders), five-state prokaryotic power regimes, neural power scaling (slope 1.04, R² = 0.88), gram-unit brain-body regressions (N = 176 and N = 277), catalog IDs and Figures 1–5 agree with the project's canonical data files.","revised":"On 24 November 2026, Table 1 was expanded to all 33 canonical rows verbatim from cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv, synaptic-to-DRAM (20,000×) and AQFP-to-CMOS (2.36×10⁴× to 1.27×10⁵×) ratios were reconciled, pure cleft diffusion (0.263 µs) was separated from vesicular delay (200 µs), neural power and gram-unit brain-body regressions were recomputed directly from the canonical CSVs, prokaryotic power states and catalog IDs were aligned, and all five figures were regenerated.","pdf":"papers/paper1.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":2,"title":"Macroevolutionary Allometry of Brain Architecture: Resolving the Encephalization Paradox via Clade-Specific Cellular Scaling and Deep-Time Grade Shifts","plain_title":"Why small bird brains can out-think big mammal brains","thesis":"Brain size alone misleads: what matters is how many neurons a brain packs in, and birds, primates and other mammals each follow their own packing rule.","summary":"The paper analyses all 54 species in the project's central-nervous-system table (11 invertebrates and 43 vertebrates across 68 census moments) alongside the 176 individual animals in the broader species table. It tests single-line versus clade-grade allometry on the exact same 43-vertebrate sample with maximum-likelihood phylogenetic generalised least squares, fits separate forebrain and whole-brain neuron-packing power laws for primates, non-primate mammals and birds, and pairs isotropic-fractionator neuron counts with per-neuron glucose budgets to explain why small corvid and parrot brains rival much heavier mammal brains.","claims":[{"claim":"Corvids and parrots pack well over a hundred million neurons per gram into their whole telencephalon, and up to nearly three hundred million in dissected songbird pallium.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.1; Figure 2","paper_value":"1.22×10⁸ neurons per gram across the raven telencephalon, 1.35×10⁸ in the New Caledonian crow, and 1.56×10⁸ in the African grey parrot (with dissected songbird pallial zones reaching up to 2.78×10⁸ per gram in Olkowicz et al. 2016)","data_value":"1.22×10⁸ (1.20×10⁹ ÷ 9.8 g), 1.35×10⁸ and 1.56×10⁸ per gram in the CNS evolution file","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (cortex_pallium_neurons ÷ cortex_pallium_mass_g for raven, crow and grey parrot); Olkowicz et al. 2016","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the text and Figure 2 distinguish whole-telencephalon densities in the CSV from dissected pallial sub-zone peaks in the primary paper."},{"claim":"A common raven packs nearly four times as many associative forebrain neurons as a domestic pig into a brain four-and-a-half to twelve times lighter.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.2; Figure 1","paper_value":"in the project CSV: raven 14.5 g brain (2.17×10⁹ total, 1.20×10⁹ pallial neurons) versus adult pig 180 g (1.50×10⁹ total, 3.10×10⁸ cortical) = 1.45× total and 3.87× forebrain neurons in 12.4× less mass; in the primary Kazu et al. 2014/2015 assay (64.2 g pig, 2.22×10⁹ total, 3.07×10⁸ cortical) = 0.98× parity in total and 3.92× forebrain neurons in 4.5× less mass","data_value":"14.5 g / 2.17×10⁹ total / 1.20×10⁹ pallial for raven and 180 g / 1.50×10⁹ total / 3.10×10⁸ cortical for pig in cns_structural_evolution.csv (ratios 1.45× total, 3.87× forebrain, 12.4× mass)","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv; Kazu et al. 2014, 2015; Olkowicz et al. 2016","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: both the canonical CSV row and the primary Kazu et al. isotropic-fractionator benchmark are reported transparently, and both show a ~3.9-fold raven lead in forebrain neurons."},{"claim":"In primates and hominins, cortical neuron count scales tightly with cortex mass (slope 0.773), and almost linearly with whole-brain mass (slope 0.946), even when fossil hominins are excluded.","section":"Section 4.1; Table 2; Figure 2","paper_value":"all 15 primates + hominins (2.43 orders of cortex mass, 4.8 to 1,300 g): cortex slope 0.7727 ± 0.0271, intercept 7.8642, R² = 0.9843 (whole-brain slope 0.9455 ± 0.0089, R² = 0.9989); 5 living primates only (no endocast imputation): cortex slope 0.7897 ± 0.0313, intercept 7.8500, R² = 0.9953","data_value":"slope 0.7727 ± 0.0271, intercept 7.8642, R² = 0.9843 across all 15 rows; slope 0.7897 ± 0.0313, intercept 7.8500, R² = 0.9953 across the 5 living primates","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (15 primate and hominin rows, plus living-only subset)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the intercept, mass span and living-only check (removing fossil endocast circularity) all match the data file."},{"claim":"In non-primate mammals, cortical neuron count rises sublinearly with cortex mass (slope 0.647), driving cortical neuron density from 8.06×10⁷ per gram in the mouse down to 7.78×10⁵ per gram in the blue whale.","section":"Section 4.1; Table 2; Figure 2","paper_value":"16 non-primate mammals: cortex slope 0.6471 ± 0.0331, intercept 7.4879, R² = 0.9646; cortical density spans 8.06×10⁷ per gram (house mouse) down to 2.42×10⁶ (killer whale) and 7.78×10⁵ (blue whale)","data_value":"slope 0.6471 ± 0.0331, intercept 7.4879, R² = 0.9646; mouse 8.06×10⁷, killer whale 2.42×10⁶, blue whale 7.78×10⁵ per gram","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (16 non-primate mammal rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Every regression parameter and extreme species density matches the 16 non-primate mammal rows of the CSV."},{"claim":"Across the six birds in the dataset, pallial neuron count scales steeply with pallial mass (slope 1.16 ± 0.17).","section":"Section 4.1; Table 2; Figure 2","paper_value":"6 birds: pallium slope 1.1600 ± 0.1674, intercept 7.9391, R² = 0.9231 (whole-brain slope 1.1572 ± 0.1372, intercept 7.9956, R² = 0.9468)","data_value":"pallium slope 1.1600 ± 0.1674, intercept 7.9391, R² = 0.9231; whole-brain slope 1.1572 ± 0.1372, R² = 0.9468","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (6 bird rows: chicken, pigeon, zebra finch, grey parrot, New Caledonian crow, raven)","verdict":"matches","note":"Text, macros and Figure 2 now report the exact same six-bird slope, intercept and standard error, noting that isometry (slope 1.0) lies within one standard error."},{"claim":"Tested on the exact same 43 vertebrates, clade-specific grade shifts decisively outperform a single brain-to-body power law (AIC improvement of 44 to 48 points), with strong phylogenetic signal.","section":"Sections 3.1 and 3.2; Table 1; Figure 1","paper_value":"N = 43 vertebrates: single-line slope 0.8317 ± 0.0520, intercept −1.6430, R² = 0.8618, AIC = −39.17; 4-clade separate-slope AIC = −83.25 (ΔAIC = +44.08; parallel ANCOVA ΔAIC = +46.65); empirical MLE Pagel's lambda = 1.000 across all 43 (0.931 across 33 living vertebrates, PGLS slope 0.6664 ± 0.0502)","data_value":"identical values recomputed by scripts/compile_paper2_pgls.py on the 43 vertebrate rows of cns_structural_evolution.csv; broader species table gives slope 0.8859 ± 0.0293 (R² = 0.8402) across 176 individual animals and 0.7955 (R² = 0.5764) across all 277 rows","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (43 vertebrates) and cleaned_biological_species.csv (176 individual animals / 277 total rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: AIC is now evaluated strictly on the identical 43-vertebrate sample and Pagel's lambda is estimated by maximum likelihood rather than fixed by hand."},{"claim":"Combining forebrain neuron packing (2.9× to 4.7×) with lower per-neuron avian glucose use (~2.3×) gives corvids and parrots a ~6.7× to 10.7× volumetric-metabolic advantage over mammals.","section":"Sections 4.3.3 and 4.3.4","paper_value":"corvid/parrot mean forebrain density 1.38×10⁸ per gram versus 2.96×10⁷ across living primates (4.66×; 2.92× mass-matched against marmoset) × 2.30× metabolic efficiency (1.0×10⁻¹⁰ W avian vs 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ W mammalian per neuron) = 6.71× to 10.73× forebrain advantage (5.08× whole-brain)","data_value":"corvid/parrot mean cortical_neuron_density_per_g = 1.377×10⁸; living primate mean = 2.956×10⁷ (ratio 4.66×); × 2.30 = 10.71×","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv; von Eugen et al. 2022 (Current Biology 32: 4306–4313); Herculano-Houzel 2011","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the packing factor is computed directly from the CSV and the glucose budget is properly cited to von Eugen et al. 2022."},{"claim":"Terrestrial non-proboscidean mammals allocate roughly 70 to 80 percent of brain neurons to the cerebellum, whereas cetaceans (22 to 31 percent) and the African elephant (97.5 percent) diverge sharply.","section":"Section 5; Table 3","paper_value":"human cerebellum 80.2% (6.90×10¹⁰ of 8.60×10¹⁰), cetaceans 22.4% to 30.6%, marmoset 57.1%, African elephant 97.5% (2.507×10¹¹ of 2.57×10¹¹); elephant cerebral cortex (5.60×10⁹ neurons) exceeds rhesus macaque cortex (1.71×10⁹) by 3.27× while remaining below chimpanzee (6.20×10⁹) and human (1.63×10¹⁰)","data_value":"all percentages and cortical neuron counts match cns_structural_evolution.csv to three significant figures","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (cerebellum_neurons, cortex_pallium_neurons, total_neurons)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: cetacean, marmoset and proboscidean exceptions are explicitly tabulated and the elephant-versus-macaque cortical comparison is corrected."},{"claim":"Across the Plio-Pleistocene hominin lineage, endocast-inferred neuron growth outpaced the 340-million-year lamprey-to-monotreme baseline by roughly 21-fold.","section":"Section 6","paper_value":"hominin net rate 0.324 per million years over 3.2 Myr versus 0.0154 per million years across the 340 Myr lamprey-to-platypus span (ratio 21.1×, noting fossil hominin counts are endocast-imputed and short evolutionary windows naturally show steeper apparent rates)","data_value":"ln(8.606×10¹⁰ ÷ 3.05×10¹⁰) ÷ 3.2 = 0.3242 per Myr; ln(2.80×10⁸ ÷ 1.50×10⁶) ÷ 340 = 0.01538 per Myr; ratio 21.1","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv (lamprey, platypus, Australopithecus afarensis and Homo sapiens rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"The arithmetic reproduces cleanly and the manuscript explicitly flags both fossil imputation and Gingerich time-scaling sensitivity."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Tested on the exact same 43 vertebrates, clade-specific grade shifts improve AIC by 44 to 48 points over a single brain-body line and persist under maximum-likelihood phylogenetic regression.","assessment":"holds","note":"Running all competing models on the identical 43-vertebrate sample with empirical Pagel's lambda resolves the earlier cross-file AIC flaw."},{"step":"Cortical/pallial neuron counts scale with distinct exponents in non-primate mammals (0.65), primates (0.77; 0.79 in living species) and birds (1.16), breaking brain-mass equivalence across classes.","assessment":"holds","note":"All slopes, intercepts and living-only sensitivity checks reproduce directly from cns_structural_evolution.csv."},{"step":"Because of hyperdense pallial packing, a 14.5 g raven brain houses nearly four times as many associative forebrain neurons as a domestic pig brain 4.5 to 12 times heavier.","assessment":"holds","note":"Holds under both the project CSV row and the primary Kazu et al. isotropic-fractionator pig assay."},{"step":"Higher forebrain packing plus ~2.3-fold lower per-neuron avian glucose demand yields a ~7-to-11-fold volumetric-metabolic advantage for corvid and parrot forebrains.","assessment":"holds","note":"Grounded in measured fractionator densities (Olkowicz et al. 2016) and pigeon FDG-PET/glucose budgets (von Eugen et al. 2022)."},{"step":"Regional allocation diverges sharply across orders — cetaceans down-weight the cerebellum while elephants place 97.5% of neurons there — and hominins rapidly expanded primate-grade cortex.","assessment":"holds","note":"All regional neuron shares match the dataset and fossil endocast caveats are stated explicitly."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Vertebrate brain evolution proceeded through discrete clade-specific allometric grades rather than one universal brain-to-body power law.","assessment":"supported","note":"Confirmed by ΔAIC = +44.1 to +48.0 on the same 43-vertebrate dataset and PGLS on the 33 living vertebrates."},{"conclusion":"Birds and primates maintain far higher forebrain neuron density than non-primate mammals of comparable or larger brain mass.","assessment":"supported","note":"Directly demonstrated across all 37 amniote rows in cns_structural_evolution.csv."},{"conclusion":"Avian pallial neurons combine compact unmyelinated local wiring with ~2.3-fold lower per-neuron glucose cost than mammalian cortical neurons.","assessment":"supported","note":"Supported by the cited primary neuroenergetics literature (von Eugen et al. 2022; Herculano-Houzel 2011)."},{"conclusion":"Plio-Pleistocene hominins tripled primate-grade brain mass and cortical neuron count in ~3.2 million years, though fossil neuron counts rely on extant primate scaling.","assessment":"supported","note":"Supported with the explicit methodological caveat that extinct hominin neuron counts are inferred from endocranial volume."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 24 November 2026 revision, every regression slope, intercept, same-sample AIC gap (ΔAIC = +44.1), maximum-likelihood Pagel's lambda, clade neuron-density range, raven-versus-pig benchmark, cerebellar share and deep-time rate reproduces directly from cns_structural_evolution.csv and scripts/compile_paper2_pgls.py. Readers should keep in mind two inherent data limits that the manuscript itself flags: the bird slope rests on six assayed species, and fossil hominin neuron counts are imputed from skull volume via the living primate rule.","revised":"On 24 November 2026, model selection was recomputed on the identical 43-vertebrate sample (ΔAIC = +44.08) with empirical maximum-likelihood Pagel's lambda, cellular scaling intercepts and living-only primate checks were aligned with cns_structural_evolution.csv, raven-versus-pig and elephant-versus-macaque comparisons were reconciled, von Eugen et al. 2022 was properly cited for avian neuron glucose use, all 54 taxa were accounted for, and both figures were regenerated directly from the canonical CSV.","pdf":"papers/paper2.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":3,"title":"The Speed-Accuracy-Energy Pareto Frontier in Deliberative Biological Nervous Systems and Synthetic Reasoning Models","plain_title":"Thinking longer costs energy, in brains and AI","thesis":"Brains and AI reasoning models both cut their mistakes by thinking longer, and both pay for it in time and energy along a measurable speed-accuracy-energy trade-off.","summary":"The paper compiles 17 empirical decision-making benchmarks (including 10 paired fast-versus-deliberative speed-accuracy curves) from ultra-fast insect reflexes to rat maze replay, crow tool inspection, ant and honeybee nest quorums, human math competition solving and frontier AI test-time reasoning, alongside all 33 substrates in the hardware table. Across the paired experiments, extending deliberation time consistently boosts log-odds accuracy while multiplying energy cost by active power. Comparing a full AI reasoning run on an 8-GPU node against a human solving the same competition math problem reveals a 56-to-70-fold macroscopic energy gap rooted in synaptic versus GPU+DRAM physics.","claims":[{"claim":"A test-time AI reasoning run on an eight-H100 node spends 672 kilojoules (OpenAI o1) to 840 kilojoules (DeepSeek-R1) per competition math problem.","section":"Abstract; Section 3, Table 1; Section 4.2; Figure 2","paper_value":"8 × 700 W = 5.60 kW node: OpenAI o1 runs 120 s = 6.72×10⁵ J (~82 J per token, lifting AIME accuracy from 9.3% to 74.4%); DeepSeek-R1 runs 150 s = 8.40×10⁵ J (lifting AIME accuracy from 39.2% to 79.8%)","data_value":"5,600 W × 120 s = 6.72×10⁵ J (9.3% → 74.4%) and 5,600 W × 150 s = 8.40×10⁵ J (39.2% → 79.8%) in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv; 700 W per H100 matches cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: all sections, Table 1 and Figures 1–3 use the exact 5.60 kW 8×H100 node budget."},{"claim":"Solving a 10-minute competition math problem on a 20-watt human brain takes 12 kilojoules — making current AI test-time reasoning 56 to 70 times (1.75 to 1.85 orders of magnitude) more energy-intensive per problem.","section":"Abstract; Section 4.2; Figure 3","paper_value":"human 20.0 W × 600 s = 12.0 kJ versus 672 kJ (OpenAI o1, 56.0× = 1.75 orders) and 840 kJ (DeepSeek-R1, 70.0× = 1.85 orders)","data_value":"6.72×10⁵ J ÷ 1.20×10⁴ J = 56.0; 8.40×10⁵ J ÷ 1.20×10⁴ J = 70.0","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv (human AIME, OpenAI o1 and DeepSeek-R1 rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the earlier contradiction between '56-fold' and 'five orders of magnitude' was resolved everywhere in favour of the exact 56× to 70× macroscopic ratio."},{"claim":"At the substrate level, an H100 arithmetic operation costs 71 times more energy than a local cortical postsynaptic event, while an off-chip HBM3 memory fetch costs 20,000 times more.","section":"Section 4.1; Section 4.2; Figure 3","paper_value":"local cortical synapse 0.010 pJ (1.0×10¹⁴ ops/J; versus 0.133 pJ whole-brain amortized human budget = 7.5×10¹² ops/J); Nvidia H100 MAC 0.708 pJ (70.8× local synapse, 5.3× whole-brain amortized); HBM3 DRAM fetch 200 pJ (20,000× local synapse)","data_value":"1.00×10⁻¹⁴ J (cortical synapse), 7.08×10⁻¹³ J (H100) and 2.00×10⁻¹⁰ J (DRAM) in cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv; 20 W ÷ 1.50×10¹⁴/s = 1.33×10⁻¹³ J in neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv","source":"cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv and neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Both the local cleft energy (0.010 pJ) and the whole-brain amortized synaptic budget (0.133 pJ) are explicitly distinguished and match both project tables."},{"claim":"Across all 10 paired fast-versus-slow deliberation experiments in the dataset, investing more decision time reliably raises accuracy (log-odds slope 0.54, R² = 0.67 across interior pairs).","section":"Abstract; Section 2.4; Section 5; Figure 1","paper_value":"10 of 10 paired tasks show positive accuracy gain (sign test p = 9.77×10⁻⁴); across N = 9 interior probability pairs, log-odds gain = (0.5395 ± 0.1420) × ln(T_slow ÷ T_fast) + 0.5725 (R² = 0.6734, p = 0.0067; all N = 10 slope 0.5695 ± 0.1905, R² = 0.5275)","data_value":"exact ordinary-least-squares fit on the paired columns of deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv reproduces slope 0.5395 ± 0.1420, intercept 0.5725, R² = 0.6734 (N = 9) and 0.5695 ± 0.1905, R² = 0.5275 (N = 10)","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv (10 paired fast-versus-deliberative rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: replaced the earlier unreproducible 25-row fit with an exact empirical regression on the 10 paired SAT benchmarks."},{"claim":"The incremental energy spent per bit of error reduction spans more than nine orders of magnitude from fruit-fly sensory integration to frontier LLM reasoning.","section":"Section 2.4.3; Section 5","paper_value":"9.41 orders of magnitude, from 1.94×10⁻⁴ J per bit of error reduction in Drosophila FoxP olfactory integration up to 5.02×10⁵ J per bit in DeepSeek-R1 math reasoning","data_value":"recomputed directly from (E_delib − E_fast) ÷ ΔI_err across the paired rows of deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv: min 1.94×10⁻⁴ J/bit, max 5.02×10⁵ J/bit (span 9.41 orders)","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Every energy-per-bit value is now computed directly from measured latencies, error rates and power budgets in the ledger."},{"claim":"A rat accumulating acoustic evidence and hippocampal replay over 0.1 to 1.0 second lifts choice accuracy from 63% to 86% at 0.12 watts whole-brain power.","section":"Section 3, Table 1; Section 3.2; Figure 2","paper_value":"0.10 s (12 mJ, 63% accuracy) to 1.00 s (120 mJ, 86% accuracy) at 0.12 W whole rat brain power","data_value":"0.10 s → 1.00 s, 63% → 86%, 0.12 W, 0.012 J → 0.12 J in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv (Brunton, Botvinick and Brody 2013; Redish 2016) and neural_sensory_evolution_timeseries.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Matches both the deliberation ledger and the whole-brain rat power row."},{"claim":"New Caledonian crows pausing 29.6 seconds for pre-action visual inspection achieve 57.1% spontaneous first-trial metatool extraction versus 0% on rapid 3.9-second probing.","section":"Section 3, Table 1; Section 3.2","paper_value":"3.9 s (0.39 J, 0% extraction) versus 29.6 s inspection (2.96 J at 0.10 W avian brain power, 57.1% first-trial extraction)","data_value":"3.9 s → 29.6 s, 0% → 57.1%, 0.10 W, 0.39 J → 2.96 J in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv (Taylor et al. 2007; von Eugen et al. 2022)","verdict":"matches","note":"Aligned directly with the published Taylor et al. 2007 metatool experiment in the ledger."},{"claim":"Eusocial rock-ant and honeybee colonies trade hours of collective scouting and quorum signalling for 80% to 90% nest-selection accuracy.","section":"Section 3, Table 1; Section 3.3","paper_value":"Temnothorax rock ants: 7,800 s (65% accuracy) to 33,600 s (90% accuracy); Apis mellifera swarm: 57,600 s (16 hours) at 20.0 W thermoregulating cluster = 1.152×10⁶ J (80% optimal nest choice)","data_value":"exact match to the rock-ant and honeybee swarm rows in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and collective_organization_evolution.csv","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv (Franks et al. 2003; Seeley and Buhrman 2001)","verdict":"matches","note":"Both collective decision rows match the field experiments and the project's collective ledger."},{"claim":"A blowfly's haltere gyroscopic reflex executes in 4.67 milliseconds at 0.5 milliwatts (2.34 microjoules per corrective saccade).","section":"Section 3, Table 1; Section 3.1","paper_value":"4.67×10⁻³ s, 5.0×10⁻⁴ W, 2.34×10⁻⁶ J","data_value":"4.67×10⁻³ s, 5.0×10⁻⁴ W, 2.34×10⁻⁶ J in both deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","source":"deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Power, latency and energy per reflex match the hardware benchmark row identically."},{"claim":"The study draws on all 975 cataloged systems, 68 sub-epochs, 33 physical substrates and 17 deliberation benchmarks.","section":"Header; Abstract; Sections 1, 3 and 5","paper_value":"975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources, 68 sub-epochs, 33 hardware/biological substrates, 17 deliberation rows","data_value":"975 systems, 2,641 measurements, 1,300 sources, 68 sub-epochs, 33 hardware rows, 17 deliberation rows","source":"Systema_Intelligentiae_Catalog.xlsx; planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv; cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv; deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"All repository counts and catalog cross-references match the current canonical files."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Ballistic reflexes minimise latency (0.13 to 7.5 ms) and energy (nanojoules to microjoules) on stereotyped triggers, whereas novel or noisy choices require sequential evidence accumulation.","assessment":"holds","note":"Directly anchored by the four reflex benchmarks and ten paired speed-accuracy tasks in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv."},{"step":"Across 10 paired biological and silicon benchmarks, multiplying deliberation time consistently increases log-odds choice accuracy while scaling energy dissipation as power × time.","assessment":"holds","note":"Verified by the reproducible log-odds regression (slope 0.54 ± 0.14, R² = 0.67, p = 0.0067) on the 10 paired rows."},{"step":"Autoregressive reasoning tokens and tree search in AI perform an analogous test-time accuracy-for-energy trade-off to biological evidence accumulation and replay.","assessment":"holds","note":"Both AlphaZero MCTS and o1/R1 reasoning chains fall cleanly along the same positive log-odds accuracy-vs-time trend in the ledger, with functional differences noted explicitly."},{"step":"Current GPU clusters spend 56 to 70 times more energy than a human brain on a competition math problem because off-chip memory movement (200 pJ) and dense MACs (0.71 pJ) far exceed synaptic costs (0.01 to 0.133 pJ).","assessment":"holds","note":"Both the macroscopic task ratio (672–840 kJ vs 12 kJ) and the microscopic substrate costs come straight from the project's two benchmark CSVs."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Extending test-time computation reliably reduces error across insects, rodents, birds, primates, social insect colonies and reasoning LLMs, at a proportional thermodynamic cost.","assessment":"supported","note":"Supported across 10/10 paired empirical benchmarks in deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv."},{"conclusion":"Frontier AI reasoning on 8×H100 nodes currently consumes 56 to 70 times (~1.8 orders of magnitude) more energy per math competition problem than a human brain.","assessment":"supported","note":"Directly computed from 5.60 kW × 120–150 s (672–840 kJ) versus 20 W × 600 s (12 kJ)."},{"conclusion":"Closing the silicon deliberation energy gap requires co-locating weights and compute to eliminate the 20,000-fold off-chip DRAM energy penalty relative to cortical synapses.","assessment":"supported","note":"Directly grounded in the 33-substrate hardware table (200 pJ HBM3 fetch vs 0.010 pJ local synapse and 0.40 pJ IBM NorthPole near-memory silicon)."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 24 November 2026 revision, every benchmark row, speed-accuracy curve, GPU power budget (5.60 kW → 672 kJ for o1 and 840 kJ for DeepSeek-R1), human-versus-AI ratio (56× to 70×), substrate energy comparison, catalog ID and figure in Paper 3 is computed directly from deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv.","revised":"On 24 November 2026, Paper 3 was grounded directly in the 17-row deliberation_speed_accuracy_energy.csv and 33-row cleaned_hardware_benchmarks.csv ledgers: AI reasoning energy was standardised to 672–840 kJ (56×–70× human, eliminating the 'five orders' discrepancy), the unreproducible 25-row fit was replaced with the exact 10-pair speed-accuracy regression, all biological benchmarks and catalog IDs were reconciled, DasGupta et al. 2014 was added, and all three figures were regenerated from the CSVs.","pdf":"papers/paper3.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":4,"title":"The Exosomatic Ratchet: Decoupling Informational Persistence from Somatic Biomass from Acheulean Handaxes to the Modern Datasphere","plain_title":"How humans moved memory out of the brain","thesis":"Human intelligence became cumulative and planetary because memory moved out of living brains into durable objects, from stone tools to clay, paper and data centres.","summary":"The paper tracks how human societies bypassed the lifespan, skull-size and working-memory limits of single brains by storing knowledge in external media across 68 census moments. It defines a dimensionless cultural ratchet number and a persistence capacity (stored bits multiplied by unpowered retention years) across stone tools, engraved ochre, clay tablets, printed books, magnetic media, flash memory and synthetic DNA. It aligns 11 hominin taxa with the project's brain-evolution dataset and compares today's 15-zettabyte datasphere and 80-gigawatt chip fleet against the 8.1 billion living human brains.","claims":[{"claim":"Storage density of human-made media rose about 15 orders of magnitude from inscribed clay and stone tools to modern flash drives, and up to 18 to 21 orders to synthetic and chromosomal DNA.","section":"Abstract; Section 5 table; Figure 1","paper_value":"0.23 bits per cm³ for bulk inscribed clay and stone (0.3 to 3 bits per cm³ for an Acheulean handaxe) to 1.8×10¹⁴ bits per cm³ for 3D NAND flash, 2.32×10¹⁸ for experimental DNA Fountain storage, and 2.40×10²¹ at the anhydrous B-DNA crystal limit","data_value":"0.23 bits per cm³ for clay and stone, 1.8×10¹⁴ bits per cm³ for flash drives, and 2.40×10²¹ bits per cm³ for chromosomal DNA in the memory ledger; 500 bits in a 300 cm³ handaxe gives 1.7 bits per cm³","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv (volumetric_density_bits_per_cm3); Erlich and Zielinski 2017 (215 petabytes per gram at 1.35 g per cm³ = 2.32×10¹⁸ bits per cm³)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: artefact densities now divide bit counts by realistic physical volumes and separate deployed flash (15 orders above clay), experimental DNA storage (19 orders) and anhydrous duplex DNA (22 orders)."},{"claim":"The engraved Blombos ochre stores a few thousand bits of geometric pattern at roughly 50 to 100 bits per cubic centimetre.","section":"Abstract; Section 5, Epoch II","paper_value":"about 1,500 to 4,200 bits across a ~20 to 50 cm³ engraved ochre crayon, or 50 to 100 bits per cm³","data_value":"4,200 bits in the catalog measurement log; dividing 1,500 to 4,200 bits by a 20 to 50 cm³ piece gives 30 to 210 bits per cm³, centred on 50 to 100 bits per cm³","source":"Systema_Intelligentiae_Catalog.xlsx Measurements sheet; physical dimensions from Henshilwood et al. 2002, 2009","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: replacing the earlier 0.1 bits per cm³ placeholder with actual plaque volume brings the bit count and density into agreement."},{"claim":"Bulk cuneiform clay archives store 0.23 bits per cubic centimetre and printed rag-paper books store 8,000 bits per cubic centimetre.","section":"Section 5 table; Figure 1","paper_value":"0.23 bits per cm³ (inscribed stone and clay tablets); 8,000 bits per cm³ (rag-paper books; 12,000 for modern wood-pulp paper)","data_value":"0.23 bits per cm³ for inscribed clay and stone; 8,000 bits per cm³ for rag paper; 12,000 bits per cm³ for wood-pulp paper","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv, volumetric_density_bits_per_cm3 column","verdict":"matches","note":"All three media densities now match the project's memory persistence ledger and correspond to a 5-million-bit octavo volume of roughly 625 cm³."},{"claim":"The world's installed active cloud, enterprise and client storage in 2026 is 15 zettabytes (1.20×10²³ bits).","section":"Abstract; Section 4, Age X; Section 5 table","paper_value":"1.20×10²³ bits (15.0 zettabytes: 5.40×10²² hard disk, 4.80×10²² NAND flash, 1.80×10²² magnetic tape)","data_value":"1.20×10²³ bits installed outside storage in both the memory ledger and the 2026 row of the planetary time series","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv (installed_capacity_2026_bits) and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (m_exo_installed_bits, 2026 row)","verdict":"matches","note":"Summing tape, hard disk and flash gives 1.20×10²³ bits, or 15.0 zettabytes at 8 bits per byte."},{"claim":"Without electrical power or replacement, today's digital storage has a capacity-weighted mean retention time of 8.30 years and a persistence capacity of 9.96×10²³ bit-years.","section":"Abstract; Section 2.5.3; Section 5 table","paper_value":"weighted mean unpowered retention 8.30 years (half-life 5.76 years); persistence integral 9.96×10²³ bit-years, sustained indefinitely only by 20.0 GW of active refresh and scrubbing","data_value":"8.30 years weighted mean retention and 9.96×10²³ bit-years in both the memory persistence ledger and the 2026 row of the planetary time series; 20.0 GW refresh power (11.0 GW DRAM refresh plus 9.0 GW storage scrubbing)","source":"paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv; paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv; planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (2026 row)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the manuscript, memory ledger and 68-moment time series all close to 1.20×10²³ bits × 8.30 years = 9.96×10²³ bit-years."},{"claim":"One human brain holds about 88 terabytes across its synapses, performs 150 trillion synaptic operations a second, and runs on 20 watts.","section":"Sections 2.3, 2.4 and 4","paper_value":"1.50×10¹⁴ synapses × 4.70 bits = 7.05×10¹⁴ bits (88.1 TB); 1.50×10¹⁴ synaptic operations per second; 20.0 W (0.133 pJ per operation)","data_value":"log₂(26) = 4.70 bits, 7.05×10¹⁴ bits ÷ 8 = 88.1 TB, 20.0 W ÷ 1.50×10¹⁴ per second = 0.133 pJ; matches the synaptic row in the memory ledger","source":"Bartol et al. 2015; Herculano-Houzel 2009; cross-checked against paper0_memory_persistence_ledger.csv and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Every step in the per-brain arithmetic follows directly from the synapse count, 4.7 bits per synapse and 20 W whole-brain power."},{"claim":"Eleven fossil and living hominin taxa show brain mass rising more than threefold from Australopithecus to modern humans.","section":"Section 3, Table 1; Figure 2","paper_value":"11 hominin rows (plus chimpanzee outgroup) aligned with the project's brain-evolution file, e.g. Australopithecus afarensis 3.5 Ma (435 g, 3.05×10¹⁰ neurons), Homo erectus 1.8 Ma (950 g, 6.20×10¹⁰), Homo sapiens (1,500 g reference / 8.60×10¹⁰ neurons)","data_value":"all shared hominin rows match divergence age, body mass, brain mass and neuron count in the CNS evolution file","source":"cns_structural_evolution.csv, Hominini and Pan troglodytes rows","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: hominin ages, body masses and brain masses in Table 1 and Figure 2 are read directly from cns_structural_evolution.csv."},{"claim":"In 2026 global executed silicon computing reaches 1.89×10²² operations per second on 80 GW — 1.56 percent of all 8.1 billion human brains — while one frontier AI cluster beats a single human brain two-million-fold.","section":"Abstract; Section 4, Age X; Figure 3","paper_value":"1.89×10²² executed chip operations per second (7.60×10²² peak) at 80.0 GW versus 1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations per second at 162 GW for 8.1 billion humans (64.3 times below all humans), while a 100,000-GPU cluster (3.00×10²⁰ operations per second) exceeds one human brain by 2.0×10⁶ times","data_value":"1.89×10²² executed operations per second (peak 7.60×10²²) at 80.0 GW in the device and power ledgers; 8.1×10⁹ × 1.50×10¹⁴ = 1.215×10²⁴ synaptic operations per second at 162 GW; 3.00×10²⁰ ÷ 1.50×10¹⁴ = 2.0×10⁶","source":"paper0_device_inventory_ledger.csv; paper0_power_closure_ledger.csv; planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (2026 row)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the earlier 3.5×10²⁴ FLOP/s projection was replaced by the empirical 2026 device inventory, resolving the contradiction with the key paper."},{"claim":"Across all 68 census moments, planetary biochemical events stay near 2.8×10³³ per second while animal neural signalling reaches 3.0×10²⁶ per second.","section":"Section 4, Table 2; Figure 3","paper_value":"protocells ~2.6×10²¹ events per second, Archean–Proterozoic prokaryotes ~10³² to 10³³ per second, modern biosphere 2.80×10³³ biochemical and 3.00×10²⁶ animal synaptic events per second","data_value":"2.60×10²¹ at first protocells, 2.80×10³³ biochemical events per second and 3.00×10²⁶ neural events per second in 2026","source":"planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv across all 68 sub-epochs","verdict":"matches","note":"Table 2 and Figure 3 are now generated directly from planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv and outside_memory_by_moment.csv."},{"claim":"A dimensionless cultural ratchet number determines whether a population preserves more skill repertoire than generational turnover erases.","section":"Section 2.5.2","paper_value":"ratchet number = (transmission rate × generation time × memory-to-generation ratio × copying fidelity) ÷ repertoire size, strictly dimensionless with threshold at 1","data_value":"not in the project data; dimensional analysis confirms bits × dimensionless ÷ bits = dimensionless","source":"Theoretical formulation adapting Henrich 2004 and Powell, Shennan and Thomas 2009","verdict":"prose_only","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the equation is now dimensionally consistent, though its illustrative Paleolithic parameter values remain theoretical rather than measured in the catalog."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Individual brains face real biological constraints on lifespan, birth-canal geometry, metabolic heat and four-item working memory, motivating external cognitive scaffolds.","assessment":"holds","note":"With the timeline framed as co-evolution rather than a post-ceiling switch — early stone tools appear at 1.76 million years ago alongside ongoing hominin brain expansion — the anatomical and metabolic bounds are sound."},{"step":"External media raise copying fidelity and extend memory lifetime across generations, pushing the dimensionless cultural ratchet number above unity.","assessment":"holds","note":"The revised ratchet ratio is dimensionally clean and captures Henrich's demographic-fidelity mechanism, though field values of fidelity for prehistoric crafts remain illustrative."},{"step":"Across human history, physical storage density climbed 15 orders of magnitude from clay tablets to flash drives while unpowered retention fell from millennia to 8.3 years.","assessment":"holds","note":"Both endpoints now come directly from the memory persistence ledger (0.23 to 1.8×10¹⁴ bits per cm³; 5,000 years to 8.3 years weighted digital mean)."},{"step":"Total persistence capacity (bits × unpowered years) still climbed from ~5×10¹³ bit-years in ancient clay to 9.96×10²³ bit-years today, kept alive by 20 GW of continuous electrical maintenance.","assessment":"holds","note":"All three figures match the memory persistence ledger and the 2026 power budget."},{"step":"Modern silicon has already surpassed individual human brains by two million times in single clusters while remaining 64 times below all 8.1 billion human brains combined.","assessment":"holds","note":"Reconciling the 2026 chip count to 1.89×10²² executed operations per second brings Paper 4 into exact agreement with Paper 0 and the device ledger."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Durable outside memory decoupled cultural accumulation from biological brain size, carrying stored non-genetic information from ~10⁵ bits in the Lower Paleolithic to 1.20×10²³ bits today.","assessment":"supported","note":"The trajectory matches both outside_memory_by_moment.csv and the 68-moment planetary time series."},{"conclusion":"Deployed storage media compressed information by 15 orders of magnitude in volume from clay tablets to 3D NAND flash (and 19 orders to experimental DNA storage).","assessment":"supported","note":"Every volumetric density in the text, table and Figure 1 now matches physical artefact geometry and the memory ledger."},{"conclusion":"Modern civilisation's digital archive is vastly larger than ancient media yet physically short-lived (8.3-year unpowered mean), surviving only via 20 GW of active refresh and migration.","assessment":"supported","note":"The persistence paradox is directly verified by the memory persistence and power closure ledgers."},{"conclusion":"Silicon superclusters lead single brains by six orders of magnitude, whereas global executed chip throughput sits at 1.56 percent of humanity's collective synaptic rate in 2026.","assessment":"supported","note":"Both ratios follow directly from the audited 2026 device and neural ledgers."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 24 November 2026 revision, every storage density, retention lifetime, persistence product (9.96×10²³ bit-years), hominin row, catalog cross-reference and 2026 compute total (1.89×10²² executed operations per second on 80 GW) agrees with the project's canonical ledgers across all 68 census moments. Only the illustrative Paleolithic parameters in the cultural ratchet inequality remain theoretical models rather than direct fossil measurements.","revised":"On 24 November 2026, artefact volumetric densities, 2026 digital compute and IT power (1.89×10²² operations per second at 80 GW), storage retention and persistence (8.30 years and 9.96×10²³ bit-years), the dimensionless ratchet equation, hominin table rows, sub-epoch anchors across all 68 moments, catalog cross-references, and all three figures were reconciled with the canonical project ledgers.","pdf":"papers/paper4.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false},{"id":5,"title":"The Evolution of Collective Organization and Multi-Agent Superorganisms: From Eusocial Insect Castes, Termite Cathedral Mounds, and Scale-Free Vertebrate Flocks to the 8.1-Billion-Human Planetary Superorganism","plain_title":"How living things team up into giant group minds","thesis":"Groups of organisms, from bacterial films and ant colonies to bird flocks and human civilization, beat the limits of a single brain by coordinating many brains, and humans did it by stacking layers of organization on top of ever-larger outside memory such as writing and computers.","summary":"The paper traces 22 collective organization milestones across 3.48 billion years (within Earth's 68-moment, 4.10-billion-year census), comparing group size, total neurons, communication channels, memory and computing rates. It shows how sterile insect castes and stigmergic trails build superorganisms whose combined neurons exceed many mammal brains, how vertebrate flocks and schools maintain scale-free cohesion by tracking roughly seven neighbours, and how human polities scaled to 8.1 billion people across 10 to 11 coordination tiers sustained by an approximately 127-fold expansion in external memory for every tenfold rise in population.","claims":[{"claim":"Across human societal stages, stored outside memory scales as the 2.10 power of polity population — roughly 127 times more external storage for every tenfold growth in group size.","section":"Abstract; Section 2.4; Section 4.3; Figure 3B","paper_value":"log10(outside memory) = −1.38 + (2.10 ± 0.28) × log10(population), R² = 0.901 (~127-fold external memory per tenfold population), contrasted with slope 0.920 ± 0.039 (R² = 0.990) when biological synaptic and genetic memory are pooled with outside storage","data_value":"OLS fit across the human outside-memory stages gives slope 2.10 ± 0.28, intercept −1.38, R² = 0.901 (10^2.10 = 126.9); pooling biological plus outside memory in collective_organization_evolution.csv gives slope 0.920 ± 0.039, R² = 0.990","source":"outside_memory_by_moment.csv and collective_organization_evolution.csv human polity rows","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the manuscript and Figure 3B now report the exact ordinary-least-squares fit and explicitly separate external cultural storage from biological brain memory."},{"claim":"A mature leafcutter ant colony holds two trillion neurons — about 23 human brains, 314 macaque brains, or 2,630 domestic cat brains.","section":"Abstract; Table 1 (Section 3.2); Figure 2A","paper_value":"2.00×10¹² collective neurons (8.0×10⁶ workers × 2.5×10⁵ neurons), equal to 23.3 human brains, 314 macaque brains, or 2,630 cat brains, running at 2.00×10¹⁵ synaptic operations per second","data_value":"8.0×10⁶ × 2.5×10⁵ = 2.00×10¹² neurons and 2.00×10¹⁵ operations per second in collective_organization_evolution.csv; dividing by 8.60×10¹⁰ (human), 6.37×10⁹ (macaque) and 7.60×10⁸ (cat in cns_structural_evolution.csv) gives 23.3, 314 and 2,632","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv (leafcutter row) and cns_structural_evolution.csv (human, macaque, cat rows)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the thousandfold unit error in the mammal-brain comparisons and the tenfold Table 1 compute offset have been corrected."},{"claim":"A cathedral termite mound holds 500 billion neurons (5.8 human brains or 78.5 macaque brains) and a honeybee hive holds 48 billion neurons (56 percent of a human brain or 75.5 marmoset brains).","section":"Table 1 (Section 3.2); Section 3.2","paper_value":"termites: 5.00×10¹¹ neurons (5.8 human, 78.5 macaque) and 5.00×10¹⁴ operations per second; honeybees: 4.80×10¹⁰ neurons (0.56 human, 75.5 marmoset) and 4.80×10¹³ operations per second; Argentine ant supercolony: 7.50×10¹³ to 2.50×10¹⁴ neurons (872 to 2,910 human brains)","data_value":"5.00×10¹¹ neurons and 5.00×10¹⁴ operations per second for termites; 4.80×10¹⁰ neurons and 4.80×10¹³ operations per second for honeybees in collective_organization_evolution.csv; ratios match cns_structural_evolution.csv","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv and cns_structural_evolution.csv","verdict":"matches","note":"Every insect colony row in Table 1 now matches both the collective ledger's neuron products and its synaptic operation rates."},{"claim":"Vertebrate schools, flocks and migratory herds aggregate tens of trillions to quadrillions of neurons without a central leader.","section":"Section 3.3; Figure 2A","paper_value":"starling murmuration (100,000 birds): 1.90×10¹³ neurons; Atlantic herring school (10⁸ fish): 1.00×10¹⁴ neurons; Serengeti wildebeest herd (1.5×10⁶ animals): 3.75×10¹⁵ neurons","data_value":"1.90×10¹³, 1.00×10¹⁴ and 3.75×10¹⁵ collective neurons in collective_organization_evolution.csv","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv vertebrate swarm rows","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: prose and Figure 2A neuron totals now match every swarm row in the data file."},{"claim":"Today's planetary collective combines 8.1 billion human brains (6.97×10²⁰ neurons), 15 zettabytes of outside storage (1.20×10²³ bits) and 1.89×10²² executed silicon operations per second.","section":"Abstract; Section 3.4; Table 2","paper_value":"6.97×10²⁰ human neurons; 1.20×10²³ bits installed digital storage (within 1.21×10²³ total human+exosomatic collective memory); 1.89×10²² executed chip operations per second alongside 1.215×10²⁴ human synaptic operations per second (and 7.16×10²² in the CSV's 100-ops/neuron convention)","data_value":"6.97×10²⁰ neurons, 1.21×10²³ collective memory bits and 7.16×10²² operations per second in collective_organization_evolution.csv; 1.20×10²³ outside bits and 1.89×10²² digital operations per second in planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv (2026 row) and planetary_intelligence_timeseries.csv (2026 row)","verdict":"matches","note":"Table 2 now reports the exact CSV column values while noting the 100-spike/s CSV convention versus Paper 0's 1,743-synapse/s convention."},{"claim":"With a base Dunbar band of 150 and a branching factor of 7, reaching 8.1 billion people requires 10 to 11 nested coordination levels.","section":"Section 4.2; Figure 3A","paper_value":"N_max(L) = 150 × 7^(L − 1), giving 150 at L = 1, 1.24×10⁸ at L = 8, 6.05×10⁹ at L = 10, and 4.24×10¹⁰ at L = 11 (effective L = 10.15 for 8.1 billion people)","data_value":"arithmetic check: 150 × 7⁰ = 150; 150 × 7⁷ = 1.235×10⁸; 150 × 7⁹ = 6.053×10⁹; 150 × 7¹⁰ = 4.237×10¹⁰; 1 + log(8.1×10⁹ ÷ 150) ÷ log(7) = 10.15","source":"Exact evaluation of N_0 × b^(L − 1)","verdict":"matches","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: extending hierarchical depth from L = 8 to L = 10–11 makes the branching law close cleanly at planetary scale."},{"claim":"Dunbar's neocortex ratio equation predicts cohesive human face-to-face groups of about 148 individuals.","section":"Section 4.1","paper_value":"log10(N) = 0.093 + 3.389 × log10(neocortex volume ÷ rest-of-brain volume), giving 147.8 ≈ 150 at the human ratio of 4.1","data_value":"10^(0.093 + 3.389 × log10(4.1)) = 10^2.1697 = 147.8","source":"Dunbar 1992, Journal of Human Evolution 22: 469–493","verdict":"prose_only","note":"Revised on 24 November 2026: the inverted coefficients were replaced with Dunbar's published equation, which evaluates to 147.8."},{"claim":"A cathedral termite mound plus its symbiotic fungus garden consumes about 1,000 litres of oxygen a day (~233 W total heat), while the two million termites themselves use ~15 to 40 W (25 W in the ledger).","section":"Section 2.2; Section 3.2","paper_value":"233 W whole-mound respiration (termites plus Termitomyces fungus comb at ~1,000 L O₂ per day), of which the insect caste biomass accounts for ~15 to 40 W (25.0 W ledger value)","data_value":"25.0 W collective insect power in collective_organization_evolution.csv; 1,000 L O₂ ÷ 86,400 s × 20.1 J per mL = 232.6 W total mound+fungus respiration","source":"collective_organization_evolution.csv; Lüscher 1961 and Darlington et al. 1997","verdict":"matches","note":"Separating fungal-comb respiration (~200 W) from insect metabolism (25 W) reconciles the gas-exchange literature with the ledger."},{"claim":"Unchecked oral relays lose 73.8 percent of message fidelity across six serial hops at 20 percent error per hop, whereas 150 people already generate 11,175 social dyads.","section":"Section 2.4; Section 4.1","paper_value":"(0.80)⁶ = 0.262 surviving fidelity (73.8 percent loss); 150 × 149 ÷ 2 = 11,175 dyads","data_value":"not in the project data; arithmetic reproduces exactly","source":"Illustrative serial-reproduction model (Bartlett 1932) and combinatorics","verdict":"prose_only","note":"The combinatorics are exact and the per-hop error rate is explicitly presented as an illustrative communication-channel bound."},{"claim":"Starlings in a murmuration track roughly six to seven topological neighbours regardless of flock density.","section":"Section 2.3; Figure 2B","paper_value":"6.5 ± 0.7 topological neighbours (optimum k ≈ 7)","data_value":"not in the project data; schematic trade-off curve in Figure 2B illustrates the empirical Ballerini et al. 2008 result","source":"Ballerini et al. 2008, PNAS 105: 1232–1237; Cavagna et al. 2010","verdict":"prose_only","note":"The seven-neighbour rule comes from 3D stereometric field reconstructions in the cited literature; Figure 2B is labelled as an illustrative cost-benefit schematic."}],"rationale":[{"step":"Individual brains pay ~2.3×10⁻¹⁰ watts per mammalian neuron plus pelvic and wiring penalties, so scaling cognition beyond single skulls requires multi-agent coordination.","assessment":"holds","note":"Per-neuron mammalian cost (20 W ÷ 8.6×10¹⁰ = 2.3×10⁻¹⁰ W) now agrees across the text and companion papers."},{"step":"Eusocial insects achieved superorganism integration via sterile castes and chemical stigmergy, assembling colony neuron budgets up to 23 times a human brain.","assessment":"holds","note":"All colony neuron sums, synaptic rates and mammal-brain ratios now match the project's collective and CNS data files."},{"step":"Vertebrate schools and flocks achieve rapid consensus without leaders via local topological coupling (~7 neighbours), propagating directional waves across up to 10⁸ individuals.","assessment":"holds","note":"The empirical topological mechanism is solidly cited from field physics and the swarm neuron totals match the ledger."},{"step":"Human societies scaled from bands of 150 to 8.1 billion across 10 to 11 nested hierarchy tiers, stabilised by external memory growing as the 2.10 power of group size.","assessment":"holds","note":"With Dunbar's equation corrected, hierarchy depth extended to L = 10–11, and external memory regressed directly against polity size (slope 2.10, R² = 0.901), every mathematical step closes."}],"conclusions":[{"conclusion":"Eusocial insect colonies evolved superorganism status independently in termites and hymenopterans, reaching collective neuron counts from 0.56 to 23 human brains in single colonies (and >870 in supercolonies).","assessment":"supported","note":"Both evolutionary origins and exact neuron ratios are verified against the catalog and literature."},{"conclusion":"Egalitarian vertebrate swarms coordinate trillions to quadrillions of neurons over hundreds of metres through scale-free nearest-neighbour rules rather than central command.","assessment":"supported","note":"Supported by cited 3D field tracking and exact swarm row totals in collective_organization_evolution.csv."},{"conclusion":"Every tenfold expansion in human polity scale required roughly a 127-fold (population^2.10) expansion in durable external memory.","assessment":"supported","note":"Directly reproduced by ordinary least squares on the project's outside-memory stages (R² = 0.901)."},{"conclusion":"Today's 8.1 billion humans and global silicon infrastructure form a planetary-scale collective holding 6.97×10²⁰ neurons and 1.20×10²³ bits of external storage.","assessment":"supported","note":"Every 2026 component agrees between Paper 5, Paper 0 and the canonical CSV ledgers."}],"inconsistencies":[],"bottom_line":"Following the 24 November 2026 revision, all 22 collective milestones across 3.48 billion years, insect-to-mammal brain ratios, swarm neuron totals, Dunbar's equation (147.8), 10-to-11-tier hierarchy scaling, mound energetics (233 W total versus 25 W insects), and the population^2.10 outside-memory scaling law (~127-fold per tenfold population, R² = 0.901) reproduce cleanly from the project's data files.","revised":"On 24 November 2026, Dunbar's 1992 coefficients, hierarchical branching depth (L = 10–11), the outside-memory regression (slope 2.10 ± 0.28, R² = 0.901), Table 1 and Table 2 compute and memory columns, insect colony mammal-brain ratios, vertebrate swarm neuron counts, emergence dates, mammalian per-neuron power (2.3×10⁻¹⁰ W), termite mound respiration (233 W whole-mound vs 25 W insects), and all three figures were synchronized with collective_organization_evolution.csv and outside_memory_by_moment.csv.","pdf":"papers/paper5.pdf","corrected_docx":"","pdf_stale":false}];
